  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why is it called the Silk Road?",
      "a": "A German geographer, Ferdinand von Richthofen, named it in 1877 after its most famous good, Chinese silk. It was never one road but a web of land and sea routes.",
      "asks": [
        "Who named the Silk Road?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How was silk made?",
      "a": "Silk comes from the cocoons of silkworms, which eat mulberry leaves. Workers unwound the fine thread from each cocoon. China kept the method secret for centuries.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does silk come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What else travelled on the Silk Road?",
      "a": "Spices, glass, horses, jade and paper, but also religions like Buddhism, skills like papermaking, foods like grapes and peaches, and sadly diseases like the plague.",
      "asks": [
        "Did ideas travel on the Silk Road?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Did anyone travel the whole Silk Road?",
      "a": "Very few. Most traders carried goods one stage, then sold them on. A few travellers, like the monk Xuanzang and the Venetian Marco Polo, made very long journeys and wrote about them.",
      "asks": [
        "Who travelled the Silk Road?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "When was the Silk Road used?",
      "a": "From about 130 BCE, when China's Han dynasty opened routes west, until around the 1400s, when sea routes became more important. Busy and quiet times came and went.",
      "asks": [
        "How old is the Silk Road?"
      ]
    }
  ],
